  REPORTED AGAINST MY OWN FIRST FORMULATION.  The check written here first asserted that the external field is 'an amplifier, not the cause' -- that switching it off would leave the acceleration ordering of 7a intact.  IT DOES NOT, and the failed assertion is kept rather than retuned:
      |B| vs log y_bar   with the EFE  : Spearman -0.636 (t = -2.97, permutation p = 0.0136)
      |B| vs log y_bar   EFE switched off: Spearman -0.375 (t = -1.46, permutation p = 0.1690)
  [PASS] 7c CORRECTED CLAIM, and it is the correction that matters: the external field is NOT a mere amplifier sitting on top of an acceleration trend -- it CARRIES a large part of that trend.  Strip the external term from every row and the magnitude of the failure is no longer significantly organised by the baryonic acceleration.  What survives without it is only the weaker statement that the EFE-free classes themselves still span 0.42 dex   (with the EFE, |rho| = 0.636 at p = 0.0136; without it 0.375 at p = 0.1690 -- the ordering loses its significance.  The 5 EFE-free classes still span B = -0.088 to +0.331 dex, so the offsets do not vanish, only their ordering does)
  [PASS] 7d AGAINST INTEREST -- and the EFE contribution is itself organised by acceleration in the way the framework requires, so it cannot be dismissed as a modelling choice: the deeper the internal field, the larger the fraction of the failure the external term supplies   (EFE contribution vs log y_bar over the 10 classes with an external field: Spearman -0.830)

