  HARMONISATION, AGAINST INTEREST.  The three EFE-using scripts do NOT use the same external-field formula:
      h9   applies G_eff = nu(g_ext/a_0) G                      -- the naive prescription
      h93  applies a boost nu((g_int+g_ext)/a_0) to g_int       -- the simple sum inside nu
      h43/h44/h42 apply the QUMOND formula (Famaey-McGaugh 2012 eq. 60), which h43's own check V2 shows is
        the correct one and which is a factor ~2 SMALLER than the naive one in the EFE-dominated limit.
  So the table above mixes recipes.  Every EFE row is recomputed here on the QUMOND recipe alone:
      Coma UDGs                    B(as published) +1.195  ->  B(QUMOND eq. 60) +1.375 dex (+0.180)
      NGC 2419                     B(as published) -0.199  ->  B(QUMOND eq. 60) -0.172 dex (+0.027)
      Pal 3                        B(as published) -0.075  ->  B(QUMOND eq. 60) +0.096 dex (+0.171)
      Pal 4                        B(as published) -0.781  ->  B(QUMOND eq. 60) -0.660 dex (+0.121)
      Pal 14                       B(as published) -0.658  ->  B(QUMOND eq. 60) -0.431 dex (+0.227)
  [PASS] 7j AGAINST INTEREST -- harmonising the external-field prescription makes the ledger WORSE, not better.  On the one formula h43 derived and validated, the Coma UDG deficit grows beyond the +1.195 dex h9 published, because h9's naive nu(g_ext) is the most generous treatment available and not the correct one.  The globular clusters move the other way by less -- and one of them, Pal 3, crosses zero, so the cluster-versus-galaxy sign split is 3/4 rather than 4/4 on the harmonised recipe.  Stated that way round because the published mixture makes the split look cleaner than it is   (Coma UDGs +1.375 vs the published +1.195 dex; on the harmonised recipe |B| vs log y_bar is Spearman -0.693 (p = 0.0058) against -0.636 on the published mixture, and the cluster-galaxy sign split is unchanged (3/4 clusters negative, 8/9 galaxies positive))

  ROBUSTNESS.  The only quantity in this table that is not read off a source or an .out is the radius at which the h50 and h51 offsets are evaluated: those offsets are averages over a radial window, and y_bar is quoted at the geometric mean of it.  Both edges are tried here, because the h50/h51 rows are the ones that anchor the high-acceleration end of 7a:
      y_bar for h50/h51 at the inner edge     : |B| vs log y_bar Spearman -0.650 (p = 0.0090)
      y_bar for h50/h51 at the geometric mean : |B| vs log y_bar Spearman -0.636 (p = 0.0104)
      y_bar for h50/h51 at the outer edge     : |B| vs log y_bar Spearman -0.600 (p = 0.0224)
  [PASS] 7i the acceleration ordering does not depend on that choice: taking the h50 and h51 rows at the inner edge of their radial window, at its geometric mean, or at its outer edge moves their y_bar by a factor of order ten and leaves the Spearman coefficient and its significance essentially unchanged   (lo: rho -0.650 (p = 0.0090); gm: rho -0.636 (p = 0.0104); hi: rho -0.600 (p = 0.0224))
  [PASS] M1 mutation control -- shuffling which missing boost belongs to which class destroys the acceleration ordering of 7a, so that ordering is carried by the pairing of class to acceleration and not by the spread of the numbers   (|rho| = 0.636 sits at p = 0.0124 of 20000 shuffles (median shuffled |rho| = 0.189))
  [PASS] M2 mutation control, the discriminating one -- the same shuffle against the MASS axis must NOT be significant, otherwise 7a's claim that acceleration beats mass would be an artefact of the table's construction   (|rho(M_bar)| = 0.475 at p = 0.0739 -- not significant, where the acceleration axis is at p = 0.0124)

============================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- the seven pressure-supported liabilities in one currency
============================================================================================================================
  WHAT THEY HAVE IN COMMON
    1. The MAGNITUDE of the failure is set by the acceleration and by nothing else in the table: |B| against
       log y_bar is Spearman -0.636 (permutation p = 0.0136) across 15 classes spanning 4.5 decades
       of acceleration, 7.4 decades of mass and 3.1 of size, and the mass and size axes are not significant
       (0.475 and 0.318).  Above a_0 every class lands within 0.33 dex; below 0.01 a_0
       none of them does.
    2. The external field is a LOAD-BEARING part of that ordering, not a decoration on it -- the first
       formulation of check 7c said 'amplifier, not cause' and FAILED.  It supplies the entire Coma UDG kill
       (+0.40 -> +1.20 dex) and 0.94 dex of the ultra-faint one; its contribution itself grows as the internal
       acceleration falls (Spearman -0.830); and switching it off on every row takes the
       acceleration ordering from p = 0.0136 to p = 0.1690, i.e. to no significance.  The offsets do not
       vanish without it -- the 5 EFE-free classes still span -0.088 to +0.331 dex -- but their
       organisation by acceleration does.
    3. Every class is a pressure-supported one measured with a half-mass or Jeans estimator, so the whole
       ledger is exposed to the same two systematics: anisotropy (free, and unmeasured for most of them) and
       the stellar mass-to-light ratio.
  WHAT THEY DO NOT HAVE IN COMMON
    4. The SIGN.  Signed B against log y_bar is only -0.386 against -0.636 for the magnitude,
       because at 0.005-0.10 a_0 the framework is SHORT by 0.6-1.2 dex for Local Group and Coma satellites and
       LONG by 0.1-0.8 dex for outer-halo globular clusters and for NGC 1052-DF2/DF4.  No monotone function of
       g/a_0 fixes both, so this is not a kernel problem and not an a_0 problem.
    5. WHAT the object is separates the sign where the acceleration does not: all four bound star clusters sit
       below the prediction (mean -0.428) and 8 of the 9 galaxies with a halo sit above it
       (mean +0.523, worst-negative -0.088), permutation p = 0.0024; the two galaxies published as dark-matter
       deficient join the star clusters.  The residual therefore tracks whether the system has a dark halo in
       LambdaCDM -- which is exactly the freedom the framework does not have.  Weakened honestly by 7j: on the
       harmonised QUMOND external-field recipe Pal 3 crosses zero (+0.096) and the split becomes 3/4,
       and Pal 3 is a 22-star dispersion, so the split rests on Pal 4 and Pal 14 -- 23 and 16 stars each.
    6. The nuisance parameter each class demands spans a factor 143 (Upsilon_V 109 for the ultra-faints, 0.76
       for the outer-halo globulars) and straddles the stellar-population value from both sides, so no single
       mass-to-light choice absorbs the ledger.
    7. AND THE FAILURES ARE NOT WHERE THE STATISTICS ARE.  The two largest offsets rest on 31 ultra-faints with
       10-100 member stars each and on 11 UDGs with 5-13 km/s errors; the two best-measured classes (258
       ATLAS3D early types, 3440 globular-cluster velocities) are the two that land.  Read the ledger as a
       statement about low-acceleration, low-N, satellite systems, which is where every one of these
       estimators is weakest.

RESULT: 16 checks, 0 FAIL   rc=0
